"executive_summary": "This study evaluates the acute toxicity of six engineered nanoparticles (Ag, Au, CuO, ZnO, TiO2, SiO2) and three soluble salts on the hatching of Artemia cysts and the viability of early nauplii in high-salinity conditions. Using a standardized protocol involving hydrated and bleached cysts, the researchers exposed samples to concentrations of 100 mg/L and 10 mg/L. Results indicated a clear hierarchy of toxicity, with silver (Ag) and copper oxide (CuO) being the most potent, followed by zinc oxide (ZnO), gold (Au), titanium dioxide (TiO2), and finally silicon dioxide (SiO2), which showed minimal toxicity. The study established a strong inverse correlation (coefficient -0.7853) between reduced hatching rates and increased larval mortality, validating the hatching assay as a rapid, cost-effective screening tool for nanoparticle hazard identification. Mechanistic analysis via DCFDA staining revealed that silver toxicity involved reactive oxygen species (ROS) generation, whereas other nanoparticles primarily caused mortality through gut clogging due to particle accumulation. The research concludes that Artemia sp. is a robust model organism for assessing nanomaterial risks in aquatic environments, offering a high-throughput alternative to traditional long-term toxicity assays.",
